{
  "file": "crates/habeas-language/src/lib.rs",
  "item": "fn[whole_comment]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This callback fills the Profile interface for every non-Rust profile.\n Those profiles all set hashes_comment to never, so Builder::encode skips\n comment_prose for every source input; plain text does not use the grammar\n encoder at all. Rust hashes doc comments through its separate doc_prose.\n The fallback therefore has no reachable behavior in the supported profiles."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-language/src/lib.rs#fn[whole_comment]` is valid. It sits in fenced citation commentary, which the built-in allows. The reason is substantive, and it matches the current source.\n\n- **Non-Rust profiles:** The css, markdown, python, toml, typescript and yaml `PROFILE` items each set `hashes_comment: never` and `comment_prose: habeas_language::whole_comment`.\n- **Plain text:** The profile in `crates/habeas-sdk/src/outline/plain.rs` also sets `hashes_comment: never`. Its `comment_prose` points at `whole_comment` through the re-export. `parse_grammar` returns `plain_text(...)` when there is no grammar, so plain text never reaches `Builder::encode`.\n- **Skipped callback:** `Builder::encode` in `crates/habeas-sdk/src/outline/mod.rs` calls `comment_prose` only when `hashes_comment` returns true, so `whole_comment` is skipped for every non-Rust profile.\n- **Rust:** The Rust profile sets `hashes_comment: is_doc_comment` and `comment_prose: doc_prose`, so it does not use `whole_comment`.\n\nThe claim that the fallback has no reachable behavior in the supported profiles agrees with the code. A repository-wide search found no other profile definitions that use it. The item is a function with a body, which the built-in permits.",
